argument on the motion the judge asked  trump's lawyer to address the second and  third ritchie factors lawyer jim trustee  trump's lawyer replied that the entire  dispute was akin to an argument over  overdue library books now the judge  described trump's motion as quote  somewhat convoluted but concluded that  quote this filing is procedurally  permissible and creates an action in  equity though there has been a lot of  pushback about the court's jurisdiction  in this particular case and in assessing  the richie factors the judge said that  the doj did not display khalsa's regard  for trump's rights so the government  came out ahead on the first factor but  it was all downhill from there as to the  second factor whether trump has an  individual interest in and need for the  material seized the judge said that this  factor cut in trump's favor since the  doj by its own admission had seized  trump's medical records correspondence  related to taxes and financial  information judge cannon says that this  amounts to over 500 pages of documents  however the government seized over 11  000 documents which means that the  subset of documents that belongs to  trump amounts to less than five percent  of the records seized under the warrant  the doj argued that the mixing of these  personal items with other documents is  evidence of a crime and can have  evidentiary value in and of itself if  trump stored his personal records with  classified materials and post-it notes  it could suggest something about his  men's rea his mental state for what he  was doing with these classified or  protected documents now judge cannon is  a former federal prosecutor and she  agreed that the justice department's  concern was valid but she said that  trump's interest was stronger and the  third factor is irreparable harm to show  irreparable harm a plaintiff needs to  demonstrate that there would be no  remedy for the damage that the  government inflicted the judge concluded  that a special master was necessary to  stop harm to trump's reputation leaks to  the press could be harmful to trump and  the judge concluded with no analysis  that the government leaked stories to  the press although the government  obviously denied this and she seemed to  ignore the fact that it was former  president trump who was providing almost  all of the information to the press when  these things happened in real time now  judge cannon said that trump could be  irreparably harmed because of the stigma  of potential criminal  prosecution quote frequently the public  remembers the accusation and still  suspects guilt even after an acquittal  though as many criminal defense lawyers  have pointed out including ronaldo  mariotti mark zaid and sarah azari uh  all defendants suffer reputational  damage when they are indicted um  not just this former president these  lawyers also said that they were looking  forward to citing this case on behalf of  their clients  and yes it would be good for criminal  defendants if they could have a special  master to review evidence uh seized from  them in the pre-indictment stage in all  criminal cases and they would have a  much better chance of avoiding criminal  indictment if they could identify the  government's witnesses and knew what the  government was thinking beforehand and  that's one of the reasons why the doj  opposed trump's motion for a special  master but national security lawyer  bradley moss thinks that it won't help  most criminal defendants because the  judge wrote this to help trump and trump  only now in her order judge cannon seems  to confirm moss's conclusion that the  rules are different in this particular  case quote as a function of plaintiff's  former position as president of the  united states the stigma associated with  the subject seizure is in a league of  its own and judge cannon came up with  another reason why trump might suffer  irreparable harm quote a future  indictment based to any degree on  property that ought to be returned would  result in reputational harm of a  decidedly different order of magnitude  of course we aren't there yet trump has  not been indicted much less indicted  because of personal property that the  fbi had no right to seize uh that and as  many have pointed out often you are  allowed to seize documents that are  intermingled with the uh the documents  that are explicitly mentioned in a  search warrant but the judge suggests  that trump wouldn't have any remedy or  any defense if he was indicted based on  material the government should not have  seized but that's incorrect if trump is  indicted using evidence that the  government should not have taken his  lawyers would file a motion to suppress  the evidence but judge cannon heads this  off and argues that a special master can  prevent this from happening by checking  the government's work in the first place  now all of this seems to contradict the  judge's same decision to allow the  intelligence community through the odni  to continue its review of the evidence  while the criminal investigation has to  stop  here the odni is uh doing its damage  assessment to find out who had access to  the sensitive information in the  documents and whether national security  interests were compromised and i suppose  someone could argue that the criminal  investigation  into trump's records would harm his  reputation while the intelligence  community investigation would not but  that seems incredibly dubious not to  mention it's all part of the same  executive branch which many have argued  is a unitary executive branch and on top  of that the intelligence review could  lead to further criminal charges  moreover some people have already  concluded that trump put human  intelligence sources at risk that  conclusion if it's mistaken  would seem to be just as serious as the  stigma that attaches to a criminal  investigation itself now the doj filed  an extensive brief explaining how its  filter team separates personal items and  attorney client privileged records from  everything else that the doj sees the  judge was unmoved by their arguments  however she held that  trump met the four ritchie factors the  judge said that rating the ex-president  without a third-party reviewer just  doesn't look good quote the government's  argument that another round of initial  screening is unnecessary also disregards  the value added by an outside reviewer  in terms of at a minimum the appearance  of fairness even if the doj filter  review teams often pass procedural  muster they're not always perceived to  be as impartial as special masters and  that takes us to the judge's analysis of  the executive privilege issue which is  to put it mildly convoluted judge cannon  says the government made a mistake by  not screening the records for executive  privilege now this overlooks the fact  that justice department is part of the  executive branch and that president  biden holds the executive privilege now  and has already delegated his authority  to nara which says none of these  documents can be protected by trump  under the guise of executive privilege  but this order hints that this is really  a dispute between former president trump  and current president biden the supreme  court's president says that executive  privilege  almost under every circumstance stays  with the office of the current president  it's theoretically possible under the  nixon versus gsa case for a former  president to have some tiny sliver of  executive privilege but  that's never happened so far and the  circumstances in which that could  theoretically happen uh are extremely  circumscribed but the judge says that a  special master must review all the  government records to determine if trump  can raise executive privilege to shield  these records but so far the judge has  only raised this issue we don't know  what procedures if any the special  master will have to follow when one is  appointed so let's talk about what it  means to appoint a special master  normally a special master would help the  court sift through materials that may be  subject to attorney-client privilege  now someone is going to screen these  records for attorney-client privilege  and presumably executive privilege but  that's sort of up in the air federal  courts haven't developed case law about  when an ex-president can  assert executive privilege so the  special master is not going to have any  guidance based on the case law itself  but presumably we'll get more guidance  from the court when it issues its final  order actually appointing the special  master and there is another complication  that goes unaddressed by the judge here  the the pra requires a dispute over  presidential records to be handled in  the u.s district court in washington d.c  44 usc section 2204 states quote the  united states district court for the  district of columbia shall have  jurisdiction over any action initiated  by the former president asserting that a  determination made by the archivist  violates the former president's  constitutional rights or privileges  here judge cannon in her order said she  recognizes that this is the law of the  pra but she doesn't say how the special  masters appointment can be squared with  section 2204. if she admits that section  2204 applies then why would a special  master appointed in the southern  district of florida have any power why  does her particular civil court case  have any power in the southern district  of florida so what happens next well now  the parties must meet and confer about  who might serve as a special master  ideally they would agree on a list of  names but that's almost certainly not  going to happen here keep in mind that  this order only applies to evidence  seized on august 8th it actually doesn't  apply to an earlier subset of documents  that trump sent to nara or the boxes of  records that his lawyers gave to the new  york city d.a alvin bragg and while many  