ings  are already segregated  from the other c's records and do not  include personal records or potentially  privileged communications with his  personal attorneys in other words the  doj has already done its review the  government disagreed as expected with  the judge's interpretation of executive  privilege and the nixon versus gsa case  and the doj has a bone to pick with  judge canada over her reliance on a  concurrence by justice kavanaugh in the  case of trump versus thompson the doj  argued thompson involved a claim of  executive privilege by a plaintiff as to  record sought by a congressional  committee which means that he wanted to  stop dissemination of the privileged  materials outside of the executive  branch and that makes a little bit of  sense in the possible theoretical places  where a former president could  exert executive privilege it would  probably be against a third party not  the executive branch itself and the doj  argued that quote neither the supreme  court's opinion and kavanaugh's  concurring statement concluded that an  ex-president can successfully assert  executive privilege to prevent the  executive branch itself from reviewing  and using its own records the doj also  argued it didn't make any sense that  part of the executive branch could  continue its review and part of the  executive branch could not but this  appeal is a risky move it could lead to  a delay that could take longer than  complying with a special master  procedures and they could also lose the  appeal because the 11th circuit is going  to 11th circuit and generate unfavorable  precedent and in a minute order the  court asked both parties to respond to  the doj's motion and it gave trump until  september 12th to file his response now  some see this as the beginning of the  judge walking back from her extreme  position before getting hammered on  appeal  but she may also just be crossing t's  and dotting eyes but you'll be able to  read all about trump's circumvention